# .gitignore for the PUBLIC repository (ledar-src).
#
# Published from the private repo as `.gitignore`. It is deliberately not a
# copy of the private one: that file lists things this repository has no
# concept of — infrastructure, agent configuration, field measurements — and
# a gitignore is a description of what a working tree contains.
#
# This file exists because of a near miss. Until product code shipped here
# nobody had ever run `npm ci` in this repository, so there was no
# `node_modules/` and no gitignore to notice was missing. The last instruction
# the publish script prints is `git add -A`.

# --- dependencies -----------------------------------------------------
node_modules/

# --- build output -----------------------------------------------------
# Every package emits both. They are also blocked on the way out by the
# publish gate, so this is the second of two fences rather than the only one.
dist/
build/
out/
*.tsbuildinfo

# --- Python bytecode --------------------------------------------------
# Two Python files ship here (infra/check-secrets.py and the patterns it
# imports) because CI runs the secret scan in this repository too. Importing
# a module writes __pycache__ next to it, so the first CI run — or anyone
# running the scan locally — creates bytecode inside a directory that is
# otherwise almost entirely off-limits.
#
# This was not hypothetical. infra/__pycache__/secret_patterns.cpython-312.pyc
# reached this repository on 2026-08-23 and stayed. It was not on the
# allowlist; `git add -A` took it because nothing here excluded it, and the
# publish gate compared top-level directories rather than full paths, so
# "infra" being planned made everything under infra/ invisible to it.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# --- editor / OS ------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/

# --- never, under any circumstances ------------------------------------
# Nothing here should ever be able to reach this repository: it is published
# by an allowlist, and none of these are on it. Listed anyway, because a
# contributor works in this tree directly and the allowlist does not protect
# what they create locally.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
*.pem
